On Sun, 4 Jan 2004, David Damerell wrote:
>
> Michael Lehotay  <mlehotay@hotmail.com> wrote:
> >The more I think about it, the more I like the idea of assigning sexual
> >orientations to the foocubi and making the character's orientation into a
> >conduct.
>
> The advantage of the existing scheme is that game balance is not
> materially altered. Anything that makes a lesser or greater proportion of
> foocubi useful without messing about with amulets of change does not
> qualify; in particular, anything that provides a compelling gameplay
> reason to make all characters bi rather defeats the idea of providing a
> free choice for people's amusement.

I see your point. Others have argued the opposite, that your orientation
*should* affect gameplay. The way I see it is (bear with me here, I'm
thinking out loud):

Foocubi do two things: (1) annoy you by taking off your armour, and
(2) dance with you, which results in either good or bad things.

If gameplay is to remain the same with respect to (1), then half of all
foocubi generated should be attracted to you. If it is to remain the same
with respect to (2), then half of all foocubi should be willing to dance
with you. This is currently equivalent to the conditions for (1).

The way my patch now deals with (1) leaves gameplay the same for straight
and gay characters. Bi characters are annoyed twice as much.

The way my patch now deals with (2) is to make foocubi twice as likely to
develop severe headaches after dancing with a bi character. Over the
course of a long game, the number of foocubi dances should be about the
same. In the early game, there is an advantage in that you can dance with
all the foocubi you meet. However, foocubi encounters in the early game
are less likely to be advantageous, since your intelligence and charisma
will be lower.

Theron Mann suggested using the kinsey scale to assign foocubi an
orientation somewhere along a continuum (instead of using 3 discrete
values). The foocubi's behaviour would be determined by a combination of
its orientation and the player's charisma. At first I liked this idea very
much. Now I think it doesn't make sense for foocubi to be more likely to
dance with players with high charisma, since the resulting magical effects
would probably be unfavourable for the foocubi.

If orientation were a #conduct, then game balance could be preserved by
tweaking every foocubi when it is generated according to the player's
current conduct. I don't particularly like this idea though. The demons'
behaviour should be more consistent than that.

The approach I am leaning towards is to make all foocubi attracted to all
characters, make the player's orientation a #conduct, and double the
probability of foocubi getting severe headaches regardless of the player's
conduct. If a player chooses to be strictly straight or strictly gay, then
this is a voluntary challenge which makes the game more difficult. You
could still use an amulet of change and maintain your conduct, though.

Or possibly, each foocubi could be assigned an orientation and probability
of developing a severe headache (regardless of the character's conduct).
If I thought about the ratios and probabilities for long enough, I might
be able to figure out a way do this in a way that preserves the game
balance for characters of all orientations. Any statisticians out there
want to give me a hand?

